;">The agenda</h2></div></td></tr>
<tr><td align=3D"left" class=3D"text" style=3D"background:#FFFFFF;font-size=
:0px;padding:0 16px;word-break:break-word;"><div style=3D"font-family:Georg=
ia, serif;font-size:16px;font-weight:400;line-height:22px;text-align:left;c=
olor:#121212;">
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Turmoil in the government bond market is r=
eviving memories of the eurozone debt crisis 15 years ago =E2=80=93 but thi=
s time France is in the firing line.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Concerns over Paris=E2=80=99s fiscal posit=
ion are pushing its borrowing costs up, amid a global sell-off of sovereign=
 debt. This pushed the gap between France and Germany=E2=80=99s borrowing c=
osts, a key measure of investor concern, to its widest level since 2012.</p=
>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Yesterday the yield on French 10-year gove=
rnment bonds (<a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001=
.8P_iYkg0_u5AycVUkiWn7G2NbW-SadK6MiFfsuSK2bCyJ8DDx32k_m-8O6-ml382ouxkny8i4g=
cD3jr5Eg2g-4SbP_hV5S6SPIzUNC-xuyYb-G5wsmDBnSfF90ktuxzRsf-tg6ka3yXxyyVUlvxPN=
nFrf3lhrr31r4OMdUJhGtKnd8P22VLzh9_y4oz-lWdi223hoxCNS5UGWnyINduru3vfoGxKsyIx=
g13O87wg6miUutAz4R8Z_HB6ukyGGwv4WSfGI7Umdf0_jFN5D9q1Ug/4uj/99TlcD6xSIShRVYP=
kHWGgA/h39/h001.AZYLYwFRBdaXw1WT6IPMRzlOOK0gWG4MSjxsB9tgg3w" style=3D"color=
: #0077B6;">or OATs</a>) yields jumped to their highest level since 2002, b=
efore dipping back as the bond rout eased.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Investors are reluctant to eat their OATs =
because of political uncertainty, with presidential elections scheduled for=
 2027, and concerns over France=E2=80=99s public debt, which has climbed to=
 a record high.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Jim Reid, a Deutsche Bank strategist, said=
 that yesterday the Franco-German 10-year spread (+13.9bps) experienced its=
 biggest daily jump since March 2020 at the height of the Covid turmoil.</p=
>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">He told clients this morning: =E2=80=9CMar=
kets stumbled yesterday as we began Q4, with mounting signs of financial st=
ress focused on Europe. In fact, the daily moves were reminiscent of the eu=
ro crisis in many respects, with sovereign contagion a big talking point.=
=E2=80=9D</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">France=E2=80=99s government did try to coo=
l the situation yesterday by proposing a budget for next year including =E2=
=82=AC43bn in cuts and tax rises.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Under the proposed plan, the tax burden wo=
uld rise while spending growth would be slowed through slashing state spend=
ing, and capping increases to pensions and civil servant salaries.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The finance minister, Roland Lescure, said=
 it was important to put France back on track for deficit reduction.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">But even with this plan, the French budget=
 deficit would only fall to 5% of GDP next year.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Analysts at ING warn that this deficit wou=
ld be =E2=80=9Cfar too high=E2=80=9D to prevent France=E2=80=99s national d=
ebt (already 119% of GDP) from rising higher.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The euro is trading close to the 17-month =
low hit yesterday, when the single currency fell by more than 0.75% to as l=
